{"st":"MA","name":"Massachusetts","seats":[{"census":1789,"seats":8},{"census":1790,"seats":14},{"census":1800,"seats":17},{"census":1810,"seats":20},{"census":1820,"seats":13},{"census":1830,"seats":12},{"census":1840,"seats":10},{"census":1850,"seats":11},{"census":1860,"seats":10},{"census":1870,"seats":11},{"census":1880,"seats":12},{"census":1890,"seats":13},{"census":1900,"seats":14},{"census":1910,"seats":16},{"census":1920,"seats":16},{"census":1930,"seats":15},{"census":1940,"seats":14},{"census":1950,"seats":14},{"census":1960,"seats":12},{"census":1970,"seats":12},{"census":1980,"seats":11},{"census":1990,"seats":10},{"census":2000,"seats":10},{"census":2010,"seats":9},{"census":2020,"seats":9}],"events":[{"congress":1,"year":1788,"kind":"statehood","title":"Ratified the Constitution","text":"Massachusetts ratified the Constitution on February 6, 1788, the 6th state to do so. The Constitution gave it 8 seats in the House until the first census.","sources":["https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R47747.html","https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":3,"year":1792,"kind":"census","title":"1790 census apportionment","text":"After the 1790 census, Massachusetts's House delegation grew from 8 to 14 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":8,"year":1802,"kind":"census","title":"1800 census apportionment","text":"After the 1800 census, Massachusetts's House delegation grew from 14 to 17 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":12,"year":1812,"kind":"other","title":"Origin of the word \"gerrymander\"","text":"Massachusetts lawmakers drew a state senate district north of Boston that critics said looked like a salamander. Governor Elbridge Gerry signed the plan in February 1812, and a Boston Gazette cartoon that March named it the \"Gerry-mander\".","sources":["https://blogs.loc.gov/loc/2012/10/terminology-in-office/","https://blogs.loc.gov/law/2017/02/elbridge-gerry-and-the-monstrous-gerrymander/"]},{"congress":13,"year":1812,"kind":"census","title":"1810 census apportionment","text":"After the 1810 census, Massachusetts's House delegation grew from 17 to 20 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":18,"year":1822,"kind":"census","title":"1820 census apportionment","text":"After the 1820 census, Massachusetts's House delegation shrank from 20 to 13 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":23,"year":1832,"kind":"census","title":"1830 census apportionment","text":"After the 1830 census, Massachusetts's House delegation shrank from 13 to 12 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":28,"year":1842,"kind":"census","title":"1840 census apportionment","text":"After the 1840 census, Massachusetts's House delegation shrank from 12 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":33,"year":1852,"kind":"census","title":"1850 census apportionment","text":"After the 1850 census, Massachusetts's House delegation grew from 10 to 11 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"census","title":"1860 census apportionment","text":"After the 1860 census, Massachusetts's House delegation shrank from 11 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"census","title":"1870 census apportionment","text":"After the 1870 census, Massachusetts's House delegation grew from 10 to 11 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"census","title":"1880 census apportionment","text":"After the 1880 census, Massachusetts's House delegation grew from 11 to 12 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"census","title":"1890 census apportionment","text":"After the 1890 census, Massachusetts's House delegation grew from 12 to 13 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":58,"year":1902,"kind":"census","title":"1900 census apportionment","text":"After the 1900 census, Massachusetts's House delegation grew from 13 to 14 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"census","title":"1910 census apportionment","text":"After the 1910 census, Massachusetts's House delegation grew from 14 to 16 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"census","title":"1930 census apportionment","text":"After the 1930 census, Massachusetts's House delegation shrank from 16 to 15 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"census","title":"1940 census apportionment","text":"After the 1940 census, Massachusetts's House delegation shrank from 15 to 14 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":83,"year":1952,"kind":"census","title":"1950 census apportionment","text":"After the 1950 census, Massachusetts kept 14 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"census","title":"1960 census apportionment","text":"After the 1960 census, Massachusetts's House delegation shrank from 14 to 12 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":93,"year":1972,"kind":"census","title":"1970 census apportionment","text":"After the 1970 census, Massachusetts kept 12 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"census","title":"1980 census apportionment","text":"After the 1980 census, Massachusetts's House delegation shrank from 12 to 11 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"census","title":"1990 census apportionment","text":"After the 1990 census, Massachusetts's House delegation shrank from 11 to 10 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"census","title":"2000 census apportionment","text":"After the 2000 census, Massachusetts kept 10 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"other","title":"Map becomes law without signature","text":"The legislature passed a 10-district plan on January 31, 2002, after rejecting a proposal from the governor. It became law on February 11, 2002, without the governor's signature.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/massachusetts/"]},{"congress":113,"year":2011,"kind":"other","title":"Map redrawn for nine seats","text":"With Massachusetts dropping from ten seats to nine, the legislature passed a new map on November 16, 2011, and the governor signed it November 21. No court challenge appears to have been filed.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/massachusetts/"]},{"congress":113,"year":2012,"kind":"census","title":"2010 census apportionment","text":"After the 2010 census, Massachusetts's House delegation shrank from 10 to 9 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":118,"year":2021,"kind":"other","title":"Current map enacted","text":"The legislature passed a new nine-district map on November 17, 2021, and the governor signed it November 22, effective immediately.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/massachusetts/"]},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"census","title":"2020 census apportionment","text":"After the 2020 census, Massachusetts kept 9 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]}],"compactness":{"c001":{"MA-1":0.218,"MA-2":0.256,"MA-3":0.267,"MA-4":0.628,"MA-5":0.093,"MA-6":0.087,"MA-7":0.083,"MA-8":0.433},"c002":{"MA-1":0.218,"MA-2":0.256,"MA-3":0.267,"MA-4":0.628,"MA-5":0.093,"MA-6":0.083,"MA-7":0.433,"MA-8":0.087},"c003":{"MA-1":0.208,"MA-2":0.57,"MA-